ect to raise more in taxes a  share of national income by 2025 than he  expected last october the tax burden is  a 70-year high um  today he's been out on the media  explaining that there's a limit to how  much politicians can magic away we're  facing a costly living crisis inflation  is in the high signal single digits or  at least is expected to be um and of  course we've got soaring energy prices  which is partly um  because a global phenomenon although i  think that the bank of england montreal  policy committee has a lot of questions  to answer on that front and i think that  rishi soon acts right ultimately that  there is a limit to how much government  is able to protect people from rising  prices but nonetheless i don't think  he's been able to pull the wool over  eyes okay this time  all right yeah let's get a quick uh  quick thought on this from moya before i  asked moyer about uh piano i mean he has  been dealt the worst hand of any chance  in decades but has he has he missed a  trick yesterday  well i mean we're seeing the biggest  fall in living standards since records  began so i think that would suggest he's  missed a trick uh you know financial  experts say that his plans will push a  further 1.3 million people into poverty  i think the thing to remember here is we  throw around these numbers we throw in  these terms we talk about this but in  real tangible impact people are getting  poorer wages are having less real world  impact energy bills are going up  every every cost is going up possible  while the amount we have to deal with is  getting less and people are going to  suffer  today didn't he inaudible said he was  sorry but he also said that he did break  the law and he'd do it again  well he said he was sorry and he said  but he said it was what he needed to do  and i think what is interesting about  the piano um scandal is that it really  shines a spotlight on the environment  created for workers over the last decade  so the law that allowed 